          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Virginia Woolf's Early Life</strong><br>\nVirginia Woolf was born on January 25, 1882, in London, England. She grew up in a large and talented family; her father, Sir Leslie Stephen, was an author and historian. Despite not receiving a formal education, Virginia had access to her father's extensive library, where she educated herself. Her family's connections provided her with a rich intellectual environment that fostered her literary talent from a young age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginnings of Her Writing Career</strong><br>\nVirginia Woolf began writing professionally in 1900. Her first pieces were journalistic in nature, written for the Times Literary Supplement among other publications. She became a member of the Bloomsbury Group, which was a circle of intellectuals and artists who gathered to discuss literary and artistic ideas. This environment stimulated her development as a writer and thinker.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Innovations in Novel Writing</strong><br>\nWoolf's writing is known for its stream-of-consciousness style, which was innovative at the time. This technique involves writing in a way that mimics the flow of a character's thoughts and feelings as they occur. She used this style to explore the inner lives of her characters, focusing on their psychological experiences rather than on external events.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
