          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Major Works and Themes</strong><br>\nAmong Virginia Woolf's most famous works are \"Mrs. Dalloway\" (1925), \"To the Lighthouse\" (1927), and \"Orlando\" (1928). Her novels often explore themes of time, memory, and the subjective nature of reality. She wrote about the inner lives of women, their role in society, and the politics of gender, which were revolutionary topics during her time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Woolf's Contribution to Feminist Literature</strong><br>\nVirginia Woolf is considered a significant figure in feminist literature. Her extended essay \"A Room of One's Own\" (1929) posits that a woman must have money and space to write and express herself freely. She argued for women's intellectual freedom and economic independence, which has inspired generations of feminists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Struggles and Mental Health</strong><br>\nVirginia Woolf struggled with mental health issues throughout her life, which were exacerbated by the death of her parents and siblings. She experienced several nervous breakdowns and was open about her battles with depression in her writings, which brought attention to mental health at a time when it was largely stigmatized.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
