            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Hogarth Press</strong><br>\nTogether with her husband, Leonard Woolf, Virginia founded the Hogarth Press in 1917. This publishing house not only provided her with an outlet for her work but also published the works of other prominent authors of the time. The press was instrumental in bringing modernist literature to a wider audience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Woolf's Death</strong><br>\nLater in life, Woolf continued to write and publish novels and essays. Her work evolved to become more experimental and abstract, reflecting her constant striving for innovation in literature. Unfortunately, her mental health continued to decline, and she took her own life by drowning in 1941 at the age of 59.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Lasting Impact</strong><br>\nVirginia Woolf's work left a profound impact on literature and feminist thought. Her novels and essays are still widely read and studied for their artistic style, depth of insight, and their contributions to discussions about gender and society. She remains an iconic figure in both literary and feminist circles.</p>\n"
              },
